'''Zocodover''' ({{Langx|mxi|ثُوكُو ذُوبِيرْ|Zokođóber}}, from {{langx|ar|سوق|sūq}} "market" + {{langx|ar|دبير|dubīr}} "beast of burden"; {{Literal translation|market of the {{W|Pack animal|beats of burden}}}}) is an [[W:Artificial island|artificial island]] in [[Salto Bay]], within the [[Sástago]] borough of [[Castejón]]. It is one of the [[Salto Islands]].

Zocodover occupies the site of the earliest Andalusi Muslim settlement in the Alpujarra, established under the [[Volisanian charter system|Volisanian charter]] of 1564 as a designated ''[[W:Factory (trading post)|fundaco]]'' ({{langx|mxi|فونداقو|fundāqū}})—a chartered trading post and reduction where Andalusi merchants could conduct commerce, maintain warehouses, and settle under the supervision of a Volisanian ''[[batlle]]'' (bailiff). 

== History ==
{{Main|Volisanian charter system}}
The charter restricted Andalusi residence and commercial activity to the island, which was then a tidal mudflat at the mouth of the [[Guadalquivir River|Guadalquivir]], effectively segregating the Bayani population from the Volisanian colonial core while harnessing their commercial networks for the colonial economy. The name ''Zocodover''—a Mozbayani compound meaning "market of burden beasts"—records the island's original function as the terminus for caravans arriving from the interior via the [[Angostura]], where goods were offloaded from pack animals and transferred to maritime trade.

The Andalusi community transformed the tidal mudflat into habitable land through a program of land reclamation modeled on the hydraulic techniques of the [[Republic of Tartessia|Tartessian]] coast—dike construction, drainage channels, and the elevation of building platforms above the flood line. By the early 17th century, Zocodover had become the principal Andalusi commercial district on the Alpujarra coast, with wharves, warehouses, a bustling ''[[W:Bazaar|zoco]]'' (covered market), numerous [[W:Hammam|hammam]]<nowiki/>s, and a network of narrow lanes organized around the central plaza. The island's population—composed of Andalusi merchants, Mozbayani artisans and free Kaftian traders—made it one of the most ethnically diverse settlements in the colonial-era Levante.

The charter system's restrictions on Andalusi residence outside the ''fundaco'' produced a densely built, inward-turning urban fabric that persisted long after the charter's legal force expired. The island's narrow street pattern, party-wall construction, and interior courtyards all derive from the Mozbayani building tradition adapted to the constraints of a reclaimed mudflat. The hammams of Zocodover, built in 1611 are some of the oldest continuously operating public bathhouses in the Levante and some of the finest surviving examples of [[W:Neo-Mudéjar|Mozbayani colonial architecture]] in Balisca.

=== Recent ===
Following the [[W:Containerisation|containerisation]] of shipping traffic in the mid-20th century, the Port of Sástago fell into disuse as traffic shifted to the [[Port of Jatiel]], and by the 1990s the island was virtually abandoned. The final nail in the coffin was a [[1992 Salto Bay referendum|1992 referendum]] which banned most activity from water-based industries in [[Salto Bay]].

The groundbreaking for the [[Castejón Skytree]] in 2009 spurred significant interest in undertaking [[W:Urban renewal|urban renewal]] on the island, and it has since experienced a boom in housing construction. Now known for its [[W:Contemporary architecture|contemporary architecture]], the island is home to a number of heritage buildings that have been retained for [[W:Adaptive reuse|adaptive reuse]], including the hammams, the former ''zoco'' building (now the Zocodover Market Hall), and the [[Casa del Batlle]]—the former bailiff's residence and the oldest Volisanian colonial structure on the Alpujarra coast.

